      <!-- Section 02: The Architectural Thesis -->
      <section class="block">
        <div class="sec-grid-head">
          <div class="sec-num"><strong>02</strong>Design Thesis</div>
          <div>
            <h2>Eliminating the research–production split</h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              Performance is a consequence of the architecture, not its sole purpose. The harder
              problem in systematic trading is structural consistency across the entire lifecycle.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="thesis-grid">
          <div class="thesis-prose">
            <p>
              A typical quant workflow builds features in vectorized Python notebooks, trains a
              model, and then hands the specification to an execution system written in another
              language—or serves predictions over localhost HTTP from a separate Python daemon.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Every boundary between those layers is a source of silent failure:</strong>
              slightly different bar alignment, timestamp rounding, missing-quote handling, or
              warmup behavior causes live inference to diverge from backtest expectations.
            </p>
            <p>
              KeronisEngine treats feature computation, simulation, model inference, risk control,
              and order routing as <strong>one computational process</strong>. Python remains where it
              excels—model training, diagnostics, and the bundled venue proxy—while everything on
              the event path runs as compiled C++20.
            </p>
          </div>

          <table class="contrast-table" aria-label="Architectural comparison">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Dimension</th>
                <th>Split Research / Live Stack</th>
                <th>KeronisEngine</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Feature code</td>
                <td>Written twice (research scripts vs. live service); prone to drift</td>
                <td>Single compiled C++ <code>StrategyStateMachine</code> shared everywhere</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Model inference</td>
                <td>External Python microservice; serialization + IPC + GIL latency</td>
                <td>In-process ONNX Runtime C++ session on tick arrival (&lt;1 ms)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Simulation parity</td>
                <td>Separate backtest scripts with simplified order assumptions</td>
                <td>Same strategy binary in <code>backtest_runner</code>, <code>PaperExchange</code>, and live</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Accounting</td>
                <td>Ad-hoc float64 drift across venues and partial fills</td>
                <td>Arbitrary-precision MPFR (<code>mpreal</code>) with strict <code>-fno-fast-math</code></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

